{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J8 — ADR amending 0035: the Jira-family sub-seam, shared provenance, and the DC horizon\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nADR 0035 §6 states that \"the first real second backend is out of scope for this ADR and is tracked separately\nby epic `be74-7832-03a8-48ac`\" — the GitHub adapter. This epic lands a different second backend first, and it\nintroduces a structure ADR 0035 does not describe: a `jira_family` layer between the neutral core and a\nconcrete adapter, with two contracts (`RichTextCodec`, `UserIdentityModel`) parameterizing it. It also makes a\nprovenance decision — that the Jira family shares one `jira` creation channel and `jira-` id prefix, with\ndeployments distinguished by `RemoteRef.instance` — which is precisely the decision PR #120 would have made\nsilently by inheriting the hardcoded literal `validate_creation_channel(\"jira\")` — today at\n`apply_inbound_records.py:211`, not :163 as an earlier draft of this plan said. Story J9's identity\nfix shifted it, and line 163 now holds an unrelated tag list-comprehension. CITE THE SYMBOL, not a\nline number: this plan has already gone stale against this exact file once. And it adds rebar's first\nthird-party runtime dependency in the reconciler, confined to an extra.\n\nLeft unrecorded, each of these gets re-litigated the next time a Jira-like tracker appears.\n\n## Approach\n\nWrite one ADR that amends 0035 rather than replacing it, recording: the `adapters/jira_family/` layer and why\nthe seam is now two levels for one vendor family; the two parameterizing contracts; the shared-`jira`\nprovenance decision and explicitly what it defers to epic `be74` (generalizing `jira_key`, the `jira-` prefix,\nand the channel vocabulary when a genuinely different vendor lands); the `pycontribs/jira` dependency, why it is\nextra-gated, and why the Cloud ACLI path was deliberately not migrated onto it; and the Docker harness as the\nverification oracle given that DC trial licenses ended in March 2026 and no official DC OpenAPI spec exists.\n\nThe ADR states the **horizon** plainly: Data Center goes read-only on 28 March 2029 and receives no new\nfeatures today, so this is a deliberately time-boxed investment for existing self-hosted clients, and the\ndurable asset is the family seam rather than the DC adapter. That framing is what tells a future maintainer\nwhether to extend this code or retire it.\n\nThe ADR must ALSO record two findings this epic produced by execution, both already evidenced on this\nticket's comments — they are properties of the SEAM, not of the DC adapter, so epic `be74` (GitHub\nIssues) inherits them:\n\n* **The port did not state what the core requires.** `TicketTransport` declares SIX members while the\n  core reaches for FIFTEEN distinct transport members; TWELVE were absent from the DC transport and\n  declared on no Protocol at all. The consequence was measured, not theorised: a DC writing reconcile\n  pass crashed (`set_entity_property`) while `isinstance(backend, Backend)`, the backend contract\n  suite, and 1636 reconciler unit tests were all green. So ADR 0035's \"the contract suite certifies a\n  backend\" holds ONLY as far as the port is complete — and, per [rebar:cc77-8120-bcc6-47e8], only as\n  far as the transport parameters are TYPED, since an unannotated `client` makes the gap invisible to\n  mypy. State that conditionality plainly. Tracked by [rebar:a357-b747-ece9-4cf5] and\n  [rebar:bda8-370d-23ad-4e36]; the exception half is [rebar:bb8c-8283-80c6-4052].\n* **`RemoteRef.instance` is NOT built.** The ADR must NOT assert that deployments *are* distinguished\n  by it as though that were existing reality. `RemoteRef` is never constructed in production code —\n  `src/` holds only the frozen dataclass definition and docstrings; the only constructions in the repo\n  are two test helpers. State it as the INTENDED design with the gap named and tracked\n  ([rebar:6a91-7429-e521-4a2e]), or a future reader will build on a field nothing populates.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** documenting only in `docs/architecture.md`. rebar's ADR set is where rejected\nalternatives live; without them the fork-per-deployment and hand-rolled-client options get re-proposed.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `docs/adr/0055-jira-family-sub-seam.md` (new — 0055 is the next free number; 0054 is taken by\n  `0054-data-driven-calibration-no-costly-llm-assessments.md`).\n  **Numbering caution:** THREE existing files share the number 0035 —\n  `0035-rc2b-snapshot-horizon-safe-replay.md`, `0035-code-review-two-lane-tier-tagged-impact.md`, and\n  `0035-reconciler-vendor-adapter-seam.md`. This story amends the THIRD. Every reference must cite the\n  FILENAME; a bare \"ADR 0035\" is ambiguous in this repo.\n- `docs/adr/0035-reconciler-vendor-adapter-seam.md` — a pointer noting the amendment and correcting §6's\n  \"first real second backend\" statement.\n- `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/user-guide.md` — the `reconciler.backend` choice and the DC extra.\n- `docs/env-vars.md` — regenerated for `JIRA_PAT`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/user-guide.md` states the DC least-privilege expectation: a dedicated service account whose PAT is\n      scoped to only the reconciled projects (Browse Projects / Create Issues / Edit Issues / Add Comments /\n      Link Issues), never an admin token — and documents `allow_insecure` as cleartext-only-if-you-must, the\n      `ca_bundle` key for internal-CA certificates, and `resolved_statuses` for workflows that do not use the\n      conventional Resolved/Done/Cancelled state names (epic AC13)\n- [ ] the ADR exists, is `Accepted`, and records each rejected alternative (fork-per-deployment, hand-rolled\n      stdlib client, generalizing the store vocabulary now, migrating Cloud onto pycontribs) with its reason\n- [ ] ADR 0035 carries a pointer to the amendment and its §6 statement is corrected\n- [ ] the ADR states the March 2029 DC read-only date and what it implies for future investment in this adapter\n- [ ] `docs/user-guide.md` documents selecting the DC backend and installing the extra, and the documented\n      commands have been executed against the J5 harness\n- [ ] `docs/architecture.md` documents the `reconciler.backend` choice and the `jira-datacenter` extra\n      (it mentions neither today) — it is a Scope deliverable and was previously uncovered by any AC\n- [ ] the ADR records that ADR 0035's \"the contract suite certifies a backend\" claim is CONDITIONAL on\n      the port being complete and typed, citing the measured evidence (6 declared vs 15 called, 12\n      absent, a live crash while every static signal was green)\n- [ ] the ADR describes `RemoteRef.instance` as INTENDED design with its gap named and ticketed, NOT as\n      an existing mechanism — `grep -rn \"RemoteRef(\" src/` returns nothing, and the ADR must not\n      contradict that\n- [ ] `docs/env-vars.md` regenerates with no diff"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785489558026098001,"uuid":"874c0720-28a6-4dcf-b328-df9d54412f16"}